    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d438">

                <pb facs="0506" n="490" sameAs="frus:frus1947v08/pg490"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.62115/2–1247: Airgram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Embassy in Brazil
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-03-10">March 10, 1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A–176. There is no inclination by Department to deviate in any degree from joint
                    commitments agreed to by all the American republics at Chapultepec and set forth
                    in Resolutions VII and XLII<anchor n="83" xml:id="d438-f1"/> which were
                    specifically created to eliminate the continuance and prevent the resurgence of
                    Nazi influence in this hemisphere. Therefore the Embassy may well carry out the
                    suggestion contained in the Department’s instruction no. 366 of November 23,
                    1946 as quoted in Embassy’s A–167 of February 12, 1947.<anchor n="84"
                        xml:id="d438-f2"/> Department perceives no conflict <pb facs="0507" n="491"
                        xml:id="pg491"/> between this instruction which is aimed at the removal of
                    those Germans considered dangerous who still remain in Brazil and instructions
                    quoted from Deptel 62 of January 17<anchor n="85" xml:id="d438-f3"/> which
                    referred to minor offenders only.</p>
                <p>As requested in reference instruction, an up-to-date list of Germans now in
                    Brazil who could be considered dangerous according to standards adopted at
                    Montevideo in Resolution XXVI<anchor n="86" xml:id="d438-f4"/> would be useful
                    to the Department.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
